00:00:15.077 --> 00:00:17.177
The Finnish language.

00:00:17.377 --> 00:00:21.277
Hello every one, welcome to LangFocus channel
and my name is Paul.

00:00:21.437 --> 00:00:24.557
Today, i'm going to talk about the Finnish language.

00:00:24.777 --> 00:00:28.457
As you probably know, Finnish is 
the majority language spoken in Finland.

00:00:28.517 --> 00:00:36.537
It has about 5.4 million native speakers 
with 4.9 million of them living in Finland,
which is 90% of the population of Finland

00:00:36.737 --> 00:00:42.717
It's one of the 2 official languages in Finland, along 
with Swedish which is spoken by 5% of the population.

00:00:42.837 --> 00:00:47.777
And there are also Finnish-speaking minorities 
across the border in Russia, Norway and in Sweden.

00:00:47.857 --> 00:00:50.477
You may have seen my video 
on the North-Germanic languages.

00:00:50.477 --> 00:00:52.197
It's this one  right here.

00:00:52.197 --> 00:00:56.377
and  you may remember that I specifically 
excluded Finnish from that video.

00:00:56.497 --> 00:01:02.517
That's because even in Finland is a Nordic country, with 
geographic and historical connections to Scandinavia,

00:01:02.657 --> 00:01:05.797
the Finnish language is NOT a North-Germanic language.

00:01:05.817 --> 00:01:08.237
In fact, it's not even an Indo-European language.

00:01:08.357 --> 00:01:12.777
It is part of the Finnic language group
which is a branch of the Uralic language family.

00:01:12.877 --> 00:01:19.037
Others members of Finnic group are:
Estonian, Karelian, Ludic, Veps, Ingrian, Votic and Livonian.

00:01:19.157 --> 00:01:22.677
But Finnish is  most widely spoken language 
in the Finnic language group.

00:01:22.797 --> 00:01:28.037
All the Finnic languages developed from Proto-Finnic,
which was spoken in around 3.000 years ago.

00:01:28.037 --> 00:01:30.197
The first stage was early Proto-Finnic.

00:01:30.277 --> 00:01:35.717
Then during the Middle Proto-Finnic period,
some changes took place in that language
and it's split into two:

00:01:35.917 --> 00:01:38.877
Proto-Samic, the ancestor of the Samic languages
(Rem: Samic l. = languages of "Lappish" people)

00:01:39.017 --> 00:01:44.437
and late Proto-Finnic, which, ultimately, became 
the most recent ancestor of all the Finnic languages.

00:01:44.557 --> 00:01:48.657
It's worth noting that Finnish contains 
a significant number of Germanic loanwords,

00:01:48.717 --> 00:01:51.377
some of them which are clearly ancient in origin.

00:01:51.477 --> 00:01:53.937
For example: 
- kuningas, which means King;

00:01:54.037 --> 00:01:57.737
- tuoli, which means "chair"
and it comes from the same root as "stool";

00:01:57.757 --> 00:01:59.717
- and kuolu, which means "school".

00:02:00.557 --> 00:02:05.417
From words like these, we can see that the ancient Fins 
had extensive contact with Germanic peoples.

00:02:05.537 --> 00:02:12.717
Late Proto-Finnic diverged into three dialects: 
Eastern Proto-Finnic, Northern Proto-Finnic 
and Southern Proto-Finnic.

00:02:12.717 --> 00:02:21.137
These three dialects of late Proto-Finnic mixed with 
each other and the resulting mixes of influences 
developed into the dialects that make up the Finnish language.

00:02:21.257 --> 00:02:27.197
Around the 12th century and 13th century CE, Sweden 
conquered Finland and made it a province of their country.

00:02:27.197 --> 00:02:30.877
And for the next few hundred years,
Swedish was the language of administration.

00:02:30.877 --> 00:02:35.057
And during this time, Middle Low German
 was the lingua franca of commerce in the area

00:02:35.137 --> 00:02:37.277
and Latin was the language of religion

00:02:37.477 --> 00:02:41.477
Swedish and Middle Low German left 
an additional Germanic influence on Finnish,

00:02:41.477 --> 00:02:44.757
which during that time was strictly 
a language daily communication.

00:02:44.917 --> 00:02:54.057
Finnish did not really become a written language 
until the 16th century when a Finnish bishop, 
named Mikael Agricola created the first comprehensive 
writing system for the language.

00:02:54.157 --> 00:02:57.437
He based that system on the orthography
of German, Swedish and Latin.

00:02:57.517 --> 00:03:01.937
And it still forms the basis of the Finnish writing system 
today, although there have been changes since then.

00:03:01.937 --> 00:03:08.677
In 1809, Russia seized Finland from Sweden in a war and 
made it an autonomous state as part of the Russian Empire.

00:03:09.237 --> 00:03:12.197
The official language and the language of the elite 
remained Swedish,

00:03:12.197 --> 00:03:17.257
but Finnish national feeling and a desire to make 
the Finnish language dominant began to increase.

00:03:17.337 --> 00:03:21.577
In 1835, these feelings were amplified 
by the publication of "Kalewala",

00:03:21.657 --> 00:03:27.657
a work of epic poetry written by Elias Lönnrot, with 
stories from Karelian and Finnish folklore and mythology.

00:03:28.237 --> 00:03:31.777
Lönnrot also played an important role 
in the development of Standard Finnish.

00:03:31.917 --> 00:03:37.317
In 1863, Finnish became an official language 
in Finland, alongside the Swedish language.

00:03:37.417 --> 00:03:39.557
And they both remain official languages today.

00:03:39.717 --> 00:03:45.937
And, with a new sense of national identity emerging, 
Finland eventually gained independence from Russia in 1917.

00:03:46.077 --> 00:03:53.837
Before standard Finnish was created, the Finnish language 
consisted of a number of dialects that could be 
roughly divided into Eastern and Western dialects.

00:03:53.977 --> 00:03:57.937
The Western dialects are the ones upon which 
Standard Finnish was largely based.

00:03:58.157 --> 00:04:02.477
From what I understand, these days, the dialects 
mostly consists of differences in accent.

00:04:02.597 --> 00:04:07.697
But, outside of Finland, there are a few interesting dialects 
that are officially considered to be different languages,

00:04:07.697 --> 00:04:10.137
even though they are intelligible with standard Finnish.

00:04:10.417 --> 00:04:18.817
This includes the Karelian language in Russia,
Mëankieli, which is spoken by a Finnish minority in Sweden 
and Kven, which is spoken by a minority in Norway.

00:04:20.537 --> 00:04:25.917
So what is the Finnish language like? 
Well, it shares some features with the
other members of the Finnic language group.

00:04:25.997 --> 00:04:27.157
Some of those features are:

00:04:27.337 --> 00:04:31.917
- no grammatical gender; there is no distinction 
between masculine, feminine or neuter.

00:04:32.177 --> 00:04:36.277
Not even in the personal pronouns!
hän" means both "he" and "she".

00:04:36.477 --> 00:04:42.617
And interestingly, Finland was one of the first countries
to grant women the right to vote. And it was one 
of the first to have a female president!

00:04:42.877 --> 00:04:46.777
So maybe gender equality is just built 
into the language and way of thinking.

00:04:47.077 --> 00:04:48.177
Hey, you never know!

00:04:48.337 --> 00:04:52.577
Next: no articles. 
Articles are the words like "a" or "the" in English.

00:04:52.957 --> 00:04:56.117
English speakers often wonder how 
you can communicate without those articles,

00:04:56.117 --> 00:04:59.997
but the context usually provides enough meaning 
that you don't actually need the articles.

00:05:00.257 --> 00:05:07.497
Next: Finish has some prepositions. In English, those are 
words showing location or relationship like "to" or "at" or "on"

00:05:07.697 --> 00:05:12.257
But Finnish has many more postpositions.
These are similar to prepositions in function,

00:05:12.357 --> 00:05:16.057
but they come after the word that 
they add meaning to, rather than before.

00:05:16.317 --> 00:05:18.417
Here's an example sentence with the postposition.

00:05:20.577 --> 00:05:23.377
This sentence means :
"The market is in the center of town"

00:05:23.677 --> 00:05:27.077
"Tori" means "market", "on" is similar to "is",

00:05:27.077 --> 00:05:32.517
"kaupungi" is town,  the "n" of the end of "kaupungi" 
is the genitive case showing possession

00:05:32.617 --> 00:05:37.577
And then after , we have "keskellä" which is 
the postposition which means "in the middle"

00:05:37.737 --> 00:05:40.817
So it's a postposition, because it comes
after the noun "kaupungin".

00:05:40.897 --> 00:05:44.437
Next : lots of grammatical cases. 15, to be precise.

00:05:44.597 --> 00:05:50.057
That means that nouns, personal  pronouns 
and adjectives have 15 different forms,
depending on their function in the sentence.

00:05:50.217 --> 00:05:53.277
We can't look at all 15 right now, 
but let's look at an example.

00:05:53.437 --> 00:05:58.857
Finnish has the accusative case, which marks 
a direct object; and that's common in lots of languages.

00:05:58.917 --> 00:06:04.857
It can be shown with an "n" at the end of the word 
or it can be shown with nothing, 
just left blank at the end of the word.

00:06:04.857 --> 00:06:06.857
But there's also something called the partitive case.

00:06:07.217 --> 00:06:15.237
This also marks a direct object, 
* if the object is only part of a whole ; or 
* if the action is incomplete ; or 
* if the action is negative.

00:06:16.477 --> 00:06:18.937
This sentence means : "will you eat the fish?"

00:06:18.937 --> 00:06:23.537
"Syöt" means "you eat" ; "kö" is a question marker ; 
"kala" is "fish",

00:06:23.697 --> 00:06:29.997
and the "n" at the end of "kala" is the accusative case
showing that the action will be completed
and that the whole fish will be eaten.

00:06:31.177 --> 00:06:37.137
means "Do you eat fish?". So again, "Syöt" means "you eat" ; 
"kö" is a question marker ; "kala" is "fish",

00:06:37.237 --> 00:06:42.617
and the "a" at the end of "kalaa" is the partitive case 
which shows that there's no completed action.

00:06:43.117 --> 00:06:47.837
There's no completed action, because there's no specific 
fish being eaten. This is just a general question.

00:06:48.037 --> 00:06:53.457
Next : you may have noticed, in the above example 
sentences, that the present and future tense are the same.

00:06:53.677 --> 00:06:58.897
That's right: in Finnish, there is no future tense.
But the future can be indicated in a couple of ways.

00:06:59.057 --> 00:07:03.037
When you see a present tense verb 
with an accusative case object like this: "...",

00:07:03.197 --> 00:07:07.697
that indicates the future because it shows that 
the action will be complete at some point.

00:07:08.057 --> 00:07:11.217
When you see a present tense verb 
with the partitive case like this: "...",

00:07:11.737 --> 00:07:16.837
that indicates present tense because the action is not 
a completed action. So it must be happening now!

00:07:17.037 --> 00:07:20.017
The future can also be clarified with a time expression.

00:07:20.237 --> 00:07:23.617
Here's an example demonstrating that
there is no future tense in Finnish:

00:07:24.897 --> 00:07:26.077
this means "I read"

00:07:27.437 --> 00:07:30.897
this means "I will read tomorrrow";
"huomenna" means "tomorrrow".

00:07:30.997 --> 00:07:35.017
and that time expression is what indicates that
this action will take place in the future.

00:07:35.077 --> 00:07:40.317
Next: there are a lot of long words in Finnish because of 
agglutination and because of compound words.

00:07:40.597 --> 00:07:46.657
Finnish is an agglutinative language meaning that
words can be formed by attaching together pieces 
that add meaning to the word.

00:07:46.777 --> 00:07:49.997
Without those pieces changing; 
you just attach them together.

00:07:49.997 --> 00:07:53.657
In English, we might use separate words
like conjunctions and prepositions.

00:07:53.657 --> 00:07:59.277
But in Finnish, special endings can be added to a word stem.
Here's an example starting with the word for "house".

00:08:09.897 --> 00:08:11.897
And a few additional features of Finnish:

00:08:12.037 --> 00:08:15.597
Number one: the main stress always goes
on the first syllable of the word.

00:08:16.017 --> 00:08:19.397
Number two: there is no equivalent
of the verb "to have" in Finnish.

00:08:19.577 --> 00:08:22.817
Number three: Finnish has negative verb conjugations. That means that :

00:08:22.817 --> 00:08:28.817
instead of using a negation word like "not", it has an entirely 
different form of the verb to show that it's negative.

00:08:28.997 --> 00:08:34.337
Finnish is clearly a very interesting language that 
might be quite different from the languages
that you're used to speaking and learning.

00:08:34.437 --> 00:08:38.597
But, is Finnish as hard to learn as people say it is?
Well, I can't tell you from personal experience

00:08:38.597 --> 00:08:44.357
but it seems to me that if you've never learned 
a language from the Finnic group or 
from the Uralic language family before, then...

00:08:44.497 --> 00:08:48.297
you'll probably have to bend your mind a little bit 
to understand how that language works

00:08:48.417 --> 00:08:53.157
and that will probably take more focus and effort than
learning a language that's more similar to your own.

00:08:53.357 --> 00:08:58.877
But, as is always the case, your passion for any language
or culture will carry you through the challenges 
of learning that language.

00:08:58.877 --> 00:09:01.837
So if you're fascinated by Finnish, 
you really should go for it.

00:09:02.017 --> 00:09:06.457
Okay, the question of the day for Finnish-speakers: 
how noticeable are the Finnish dialects today?

00:09:06.497 --> 00:09:09.797
Can you tell where a Finnish speaker is from, 
just by hearing them speak?

00:09:10.037 --> 00:09:12.357
And then, different question for people who studied Finnish:

00:09:12.497 --> 00:09:16.637
How challenging is Finnish to learn? 
Did you find it quite tough or did you just find it different?

00:09:16.797 --> 00:09:18.857
Let us know in the comments down below.

00:09:18.917 --> 00:09:22.037
Thanks again to all my Patreon supporters 
